            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Arabic for old age, then the Arabic for a nanny."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: والد",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "والد — wālid — \"a parent; a father\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "سافر والدي إلى المدينة. sāfara wālidī ilā al-madīna. My father travelled to the city."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "الوالدان means the parents; والدة is a mother."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "والد (wālid) is built on the native Arabic root w-l-d, \"to give birth\" (Lane). Walad, a boy, and mīlād, birth, which you know, share the root."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
